  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Severe thunderstorms are possible Thursday amidst\nincreasingly warm and humid conditions.\n\nHigh pressure is pushing off the coast this afternoon, resulting\nin locally gusty southerly winds and temperatures pushing into\nthe mid to upper 80s. Some models continue to show a few showers\npopping in southwestern portions of the CWA later this afternoon\nand evening, but so far cumulus appear shallow on satellite\nimagery.\n\nMoisture return increases overnight along a northward-lifting\nwarm front, which could result in some isolated to scattered \nshowers and storms. There's not a lot of model agreement on\nwhere or when (or even if) this convection develops, although\nelevated instability does gradually advect northward. There is\nslightly more consensus closer to the Chesapeake Bay on either\nside of sunrise, so that is where the mentionable chances reside\nfor now.\n\nMore notable forcing arrives Thursday as an upper level trough \nand surface cold front approach from the northwest. However, \nthese forcing features may be somewhat displaced from the local \narea during peak diurnal heating. The front will lag to our\nnorthwest, while the strongest forcing with the trough passes \nto the north. A secondary shortwave may approach but not until \nthe evening. Thus there remains some uncertainty in the coverage\nand timing of any showers and thunderstorms, although there is\nsome model consensus on development along a surface trough\nbetween the I-95 and I-81 corridors by mid afternoon. Residual \ncloud cover and any leftover morning showers could put a damper \non instability. Then, especially for areas near and east of \nI-95, convection may not arrive until after sunset, when it \ncould be weakening. However, if everything does come together, \nmoderate instability combined with deep-layer shear of around \n25-35 kt could result in some severe thunderstorms. Damaging\nwinds are the primary threat. Moderate mid level lapse rates, \nstrong winds in the upper atmosphere, and relatively light low \nlevel winds could also result in some severe level hail. \nWhile there is some low level curvature to hodographs, it's \nunclear if there will be sufficient shear for a tornado threat. \nWe will have to monitor heavy rain potential as well, since \nrelatively unidirectional flow relative to the surface trough \ncould result in some training (hinted at by splotches of higher \nQPF in some CAMs). At the moment, this threat appears higher to \nour north, and precipitable water values and warm cloud depths \nare relatively modest. Thus no Flood Watches are planned at this\ntime. While some showers and storms may linger into the night, \nthe severe weather threat should be winding down by mid to late \nevening.\n\nThe cold front may bisect the area by Friday morning while the\nmid/upper level trough axis remains to the west. Some showers\nand thunderstorms will continue to be possible near and south of\nthe front, although the risk for strong thunderstorms is lower.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Drier conditions heading into the weekend, with \nincreasingly hot & humid conditions at the start of next week.\n\nHeights begin to rise Saturday as an upper level low drifts\neastward into northern Quebec. Surface high pressure will build\nfrom the Great Lakes toward the northern Mid Atlantic, which\nshould push the cold front south enough to keep most areas dry.\nHowever, some convergence along the Appalachians could result in\nan isolated afternoon shower. Temperatures remain seasonable \nduring this period, with daily highs ranging in the mid-to- \nupper 80s during the day.\n\nTowards the start of next week, broad upper-level ridging starts to \nbuild over the Southeast. Combined with relatively moist air aloft, \nheat & humidity here will start to feel like peak summertime \nconditions once again. Temperatures rising to the lower to mid\n90s combined with increasing dew points may result in heat index\nvalues in the upper 90s to just above 100. 850 mb temp \nanomalies show this pattern only continuing to build in the \nlong- term, which may lead to a warmer-than- average start to \nSeptember should this pattern persist. Thunderstorm chances \ncould gradually increase during this period as ridging \ntransitions offshore, although forcing appears minimal through \nat least Tuesday. Regardless, anticipate a return to summertime \nheat & humidity here in the Mid- Atlantic.",
